					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/catalinacobalt" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2041993); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2041993&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606380088" alt="catalinacobalt" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2041993)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/catalinacobalt" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2041993); return false;" >catalinacobalt</a></span> <span class="odate time_1569830061 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Sep 2019 07:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4381914">
									

<p>It doesn't seem to me that 4824 contains -1 at all, as -1 is stated to be capable of destroying 4824 at will. It could leave, but chooses not to; this does not sound like Hiemal-type containment to me.</p>
<p>-1 does not seem to contain 4824 so much as actively maintains its existence. This is not containment, it is perpetuation. If removing the influence of -1 instead caused the sandstorm wall to expand uncontrollably and exponentially, then I could see justification for Hiemal; as the anomaly stands, it really does read like Euclid. -1 may leave anytime, chooses not to, and does not seem to be actively hostile.</p>
<p>From the Hiemal Class Discussion thread:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In essence, this class would be used for anomalies which, themselves, combat or suppress the effects of another anomaly. Containing or neutralizing the Hiemal class object results in that other anomaly surfacing.</p>
</blockquote>

